# fastmode-sst.ti59
#
# The shortest known way into the TI-59 "fast mode", found by tracing the ROM
# with the headless harness (tools/headless/, 2026-10-17). Compare
# examples/fastmode.ti59 (the St flg trick of TI PPC Notes V9N3P5) and
# examples/calendar-08-acosta.ti59 (the "hi2" entry); rom/TI59-ROM-Guide.md
# § "Fast mode" explains all known entry methods side by side.
#
# The program computes 69! into R00, the same job as examples/fastmode.ti59.
# Loading the file runs the entry sequence automatically (KEYSTROKES below).
# By hand:
#
#   1 + 2 EE 12 +/- =    X := 1.000000000002 exactly
#   GTO 007              position at the St flg step
#   SST                  execute St flg: its flag operand is now pending
#   0                    any digit: the first of the two operand digits
#   STO                  any column-2 key (STO, EE, x<>t, INV, B) ends it
#
# The calculation starts at once in fast mode and stops showing 69!
# (1.7112245 98). For comparison, normal mode: GTO 008 R/S takes about 75%
# longer (headless measurement: 298k vs 518k instruction cycles).
#
# HOW IT WORKS
#
# 1. SST executes the St flg at step 007. Inside a program an operand is fed
#    as two digit keys, so St flg now waits for two digits.
# 2. The typed digit is the first. STO, a non-digit key, ends the argument
#    early. Its key-encode (ROM 0x0788) leaves STO's column bit (column 2) in
#    KR. St flg's continuation ORs bits into KR to build its dispatch
#    address, so it lands at 0x0B34 instead of FLAG_OP (0x0B14). From there a
#    second offset dispatch ends in the HIR STO code (0x11D9) with register
#    0, which writes X's digit image into SCOM[0]. Any digit works, and only
#    the five column-2 keys do (tested for every key).
# 3. X = 1.000000000002 has the image 1000000000002000. Its nibble 3 becomes
#    the Prg Src Flag (2) and nibbles 4-7 the step counter (000). The
#    nibble-15 "1" also sets user flag 4. A non-zero source flag makes the
#    run loop skip the display rebuild and the R/S and GTO polls. The value
#    2 selects neither the module (bit 0) nor the constant-ROM table
#    (bit 3), so the steps are still fetched from RAM.
# 4. The jump to step 000 does not reload the program-register cache
#    (SCOM[10]). Steps 001-007 therefore execute as 00 from the drained
#    cache, and the program proper starts at step 008. That is why St flg
#    sits at the last byte of a register (step 8k+7) and steps 000-006 are
#    left empty.
# 5. Fast mode ends at the RST at step 031, which clears the step counter and
#    the Prg Src Flag. The program then continues in normal mode at step 000:
#    RCL 00, R/S. Steps 000-002 are free for this because on entry the run
#    starts at 001 and 001-007 come from the drained cache.
#    Two traps when leaving fast mode, both because it skips the display
#    formatter (DISP_FORMAT): a plain R/S stops with the source flag still 2
#    and the display stays dark, even after CLR. RCL 00 RST (recalling the
#    result while still in fast mode) leaves an unformatted display image
#    that makes the redisplay after RST loop forever (ROM 0x030A-0x030E).
#    examples/fastmode.ti59 avoids the second trap with CLR +/- RST.
#
# Unlike examples/fastmode.ti59 no partition-end halt is needed. Unlike the
# Acosta calendar no pseudo-keycode has to be manufactured. A program cannot
# trigger this on its own: a sweep of all 10,000 "St flg kk kk" programs
# found none that works, because stored operands always arrive as two
# decimal digits.

MODEL: TI-59

PROGRAM:
000  43 RCL     # reached through the RST at step 031, in normal mode again:
001  00  00     # show the result …
002  91 R/S     # … and stop. (On entry steps 001-007 run from the drained cache.)
007  86 STF     # the entry trigger; its operand is supplied from the keyboard
008  25 CLR     # fast-mode program starts here
009  00  0
010  32 X<>T    # t := 0
011  06  6
012  09  9
013  42 STO
014  00  00     # R00 := 69 (product)
015  42 STO
016  01  01     # R01 := 69 (counter)
017  01  1      # loop: counter - 1
018  94 +/-
019  44 SUM
020  01  01
021  43 RCL
022  01  01
023  67 EQ      # counter = 0 → done (step 031)
024  00  00
025  31  31
026  49 PRD
027  00  00     # R00 *= counter
028  61 GTO
029  00  00
030  17  17     # → step 017
031  81 RST     # leave fast mode (Prg Src Flag 0) and go to step 000

KEYSTROKES:
82          # 1
85          # +
83          # 2
52          # EE
82          # 1
83          # 2
94          # +/-
95          # =      X = 1.000000000002
61          # GTO
92          # 0
92          # 0
62          # 7      → step 007
41          # SST    St flg executes, operand pending
92          # 0      first operand digit
42          # STO    column-2 key: fast mode starts
